<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Release on KevCaz's Website</title><link>https://kevcaz.github.io/tags/release/</link><description>Recent content in Release on KevCaz's Website</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><copyright>Content under CC0 1.0 Universal unless otherwise specified.</copyright><lastBuildDate>Tue, 04 Jun 2024 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://kevcaz.github.io/tags/release/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>From Mantic Minotaur to Noble Numbat</title><link>https://kevcaz.github.io/notes/2024/05/2024-05-30_noble_numbat/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Jun 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://kevcaz.github.io/notes/2024/05/2024-05-30_noble_numbat/</guid><description>&lt;center&gt;
 &lt;figure&gt;
 &lt;img class="mg-bot" src="./assets/noble_numbat.png" width="50%" &gt;
 
 &lt;figcaption&gt;&lt;div class="highlight justify"&gt;&lt;i class="fas fa-info-circle" aria-hidden&gt;&lt;/i&gt; Screenshot of my current desktop wallpaper, &lt;em&gt;Little numbat boy&lt;/em&gt; by @azskalt, see &lt;a href="https://ubuntu.com/blog/the-coronation-of-a-new-mascot-noble-numbat"&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Coronation of a New Mascot: Noble Numbat&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/figcaption&gt;
 
 &lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;/center&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a &lt;a href="https://lists.ubuntu.com/archives/ubuntu-devel-announce/2024-March/001343.htm"&gt;freezing phase&lt;/a&gt; that started last March, Ubuntu 24.04 codenamed &lt;strong&gt;Noble Numbat&lt;/strong&gt; was released last April. It comes with a great deal of new features, listed in the release &lt;a href="https://discourse.ubuntu.com/t/ubuntu-24-04-lts-noble-numbat-release-notes/39890"&gt;release notes&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As a Mantic Minotaur user, the option to update to Noble Numbat was presented in May, in a window that popped up after a regular update. At first it did not work, so I hit &lt;code&gt;sudo do-release-upgrade&lt;/code&gt; to better understand the problem. I quickly figured out that that the issue stems from a package that I set on hold. After unholding it, the updating process worked like a charm.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>From Lunar Lobster to Mantic Minotaur</title><link>https://kevcaz.github.io/notes/2023/11/2023-11-05_from_lunar_lobster/</link><pubDate>Sun, 05 Nov 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://kevcaz.github.io/notes/2023/11/2023-11-05_from_lunar_lobster/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;I have recently decided to upgrade to the most recent Ubuntu release on my Dell Precision machine, in other words, I wanted to get closer to &lt;a href="https://ubuntu.com/about/release-cycle"&gt;Ubuntu&amp;rsquo;s release cycle&lt;/a&gt;. Previously, I would reinstall the latest long time release (LTS) every time a new update pops up (see &lt;a href="https://ubuntu.com/server/docs/upgrade-introduction"&gt;the documentation&lt;/a&gt;).
About 3 months ago, I switched to Ubuntu 23.04 (Lunar Lobster) and yesterday I upgraded to the October&amp;rsquo;s release, &lt;a href="https://ubuntu.com/blog/ubuntu-desktop-23-10-mantic-minotaur-deep-dive"&gt;Mantic Minotaur (23.10)&lt;/a&gt;.
The two upgrades were seamless, I just had to change a setting in the update-manager (see &lt;a href="https://help.ubuntu.com/community/LunarUpgrades"&gt;this&lt;/a&gt;) and confirm the upgrade when I got notified. Once the installation completed, I had to edit a couple of source files for which only the latest LTS is available (22.04, a.k.a Jammy Jellyfish).
Here is my current configuration in a nutshell:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>From Bullseye to Bookworm</title><link>https://kevcaz.github.io/notes/2021/08/2021-08-29_from_bullseye_to/</link><pubDate>Sun, 29 Aug 2021 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://kevcaz.github.io/notes/2021/08/2021-08-29_from_bullseye_to/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Two weeks ago, &lt;a href="https://wiki.debian.org/DebianBullseye"&gt;Bullseye&lt;/a&gt; became the new stable Debian release. As I prefer using Debian testing, today, I jumped on Debian 12 codenamed &lt;a href="https://wiki.debian.org/DebianBookworm"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bookworm&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. To do so, I simply updated my &lt;a href="https://wiki.debian.org/SourcesList"&gt;source list&lt;/a&gt; by replacing instances of &lt;code&gt;bullseye&lt;/code&gt; by &lt;code&gt;bookworm&lt;/code&gt;, and then I used the combo
&lt;code&gt;apt update&lt;/code&gt;/ &lt;code&gt;apt upgrade&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div style="color:#575279;background-color:#faf4ed;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;
&lt;table style="border-spacing:0;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="vertical-align:top;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;"&gt;
